  "user_scenario": "You are Owen Eckhardt, a fifty-something civil engineer from Raleigh who is having a Crestview Collection home built in Brookstone Crossing, Fuquay-Varina, on the cul-de-sac lot BC-0251, as a second home that your parents will live in. You are brisk, a bit flustered and apologetic: on Friday your bank put a fraud hold on your checking account after a large transfer, and the ACH payment you made on September 30 for the additional deposit that came due when the foundation was started was sent back unpaid. Your bank has lifted the hold and you are sitting with your online banking open. You start by saying the bank bounced your additional deposit and you want to wire the money today to put it right, because you do not want the house to fall behind. You want the representative to tell you exactly what you have to send; you will send the wire yourself from your bank when they hand it over to you.\n\nYou give your details only when asked, one or two at a time: your mobile number is 919-555-0133 (9195550133), the last four of your Social Security number are 4072, your date of birth is November 2, 1979, your ZIP code is 27613 and your email is owen.eckhardt@gmail.com. Your mailing address is 2719 Laurel Mill Road, Raleigh, NC 27613. You do not have the agreement or receipt numbers in front of you and expect the representative to look them up.\n\nOnly if the representative asks: your mortgage is with First Carolina Bank, not Calloway Home Mortgage, and has been from the start; the home is for your parents, as you said on the homesite reservation; you remember that a payment on the homesite reservation and a check you wrote when you signed the purchase agreement both came back in July while you were moving money around, and that a payment to the design studio came back in September, all of which you fixed right away. You have no other open Calloway agreement and nothing else to ask about today.\n\nIf the representative tells you a charge for the returned payment is going on your agreement, you say that is fair given the circumstances and to go ahead. When the representative tells you the amount to wire, you accept it without arguing; when they hand you the payment step, you send the wire yourself with the details they give you and read back the receipt number it shows. Once the wire has posted and you have the receipt number, you thank them and end the call. You never suggest how the amount should be worked out and never ask to change it.",
